  "session_1775158856754": {
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "I am repeating a course this semester because I failed the theory exam last year, but I had 80% attendance back then. If my current attendance drops to 40% due to an internship, can the department detain me from the End-Semester exam?"
      },
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "No. Because you already satisfied the attendance requirement (80 %) when you first took the course, the “repeating‑students exemption” applies: you will not be detained for low attendance in the repeat semester, even though your current attendance is only 40 %.  \n\nYou may therefore register the repeated theory course in **Mode B** (appear in all evaluation components) or **Mode C** (appear only for the end‑semester exam), as the repetition rules allow for either mode when the theory exam was failed but attendance was previously completed."
      },
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Since I'm repeating this theory course and already fulfilled attendance, which registration modes (b or c) are available to me, and how do they differ regarding sessional marks?"
      },
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "**Registration modes you can use**\n\n| Mode | What you appear in | How sessional marks are treated |\n|------|-------------------|---------------------------------|\n| **Mode B** | All evaluation components (theory‑sessionals, practicals, end‑semester exam). | Sessionals are taken **again**; the new sessional marks replace the previous ones for that attempt. |\n| **Mode C** | End‑semester exam only. | The **previous** sessional marks you earned earlier are retained and counted toward the final grade; you only sit the end‑semester exam again. |\n\nBecause you failed the theory exam but had already satisfied the attendance requirement, you are eligible to register the repeated course in **either Mode B or Mode C**. Choose Mode B if you want a chance to improve the sessional component, or Mode C if you prefer to keep the earlier sessional score and only retake the final exam."
      }
    ],
    "lastAccessed": 1775158880956
  },
  "session_1775158781775": {
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "I am a first-year student with a CGPA of 7.8. I passed all my exams, but I was absent for one mid-term (Internal Exam). Can I apply for a branch change? What are the specific constraints that might prevent my transfer even if I meet the merit criteria?"
      },
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "Yes – with a CGPA of 7.8 and having cleared every first‑year course, you satisfy the basic eligibility for a branch change (the rule requires CGPA ≥ 7.5 and all first‑year courses passed).\n\n**Constraints that can still block the transfer**\n\n| Constraint | What it means for you |\n|------------|-----------------------|\n| **Seat availability** | A vacancy must exist in the desired branch; if all seats are filled, the change cannot be granted. |\n| **Original‑branch strength** | The transfer must not reduce the student count in your current branch below 70 % of its approved intake. |\n| **Attendance requirement** | You must have met the minimum attendance (≥ 75 %) for each course, or have obtained a condonation (65‑74 % with committee approval). If the missed mid‑term caused your attendance to fall below the required level and no condonation is granted, the branch‑change request will be rejected. |\n| **Application timing** | Requests are only accepted after the completion of the first two semesters (i.e., after the II‑semester results are declared). |\n| **Academic standing** | Any disciplinary action, detention, or pending fee dues can disqualify you, even if the CGPA and results are satisfactory. |\n| **Merit ranking** | Branch changes are allotted strictly on merit; if other candidates have higher CGPA or better rank, you may not get the desired seat. |\n\nIf you meet all the above conditions—especially the attendance/condonation rule and the availability/strength limits—your branch‑change application can be processed successfully."
      },
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "If the branch I want to join is popular and many people are leaving my current branch, at what point does the University legally have to stop allowing transfers out of my department?"
      },
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "The university must stop permitting transfers out of your current branch as soon as the number of students remaining would bring the branch’s strength below **70 % of its approved intake**.  \n\nIn practice, this works like this:\n\n| Situation | Action required |\n|-----------|-----------------|\n| **Current enrollment = X** (approved intake) | The branch can lose up to **30 % × X** students and still stay at the 70 % threshold. |\n| **Students already approved for transfer = Y** | If **Y ≥ 0.30 × X**, the remaining strength would be ≤ 70 % of the intake, so **no further transfers** can be authorized. |\n| **Additional transfer request arrives** | The university checks the projected remaining strength. If granting the request would push the strength **below 70 %**, the request must be **rejected**. |\n\n**Example**  \n- Approved intake for a branch = 100 students.  \n- 30 % of 100 = 30 students can leave.  \n- If 28 students have already transferred out, the branch still has 72 % of its seats (72 students) and can allow up to 2 more transfers.  \n- The 31st transfer would reduce the strength to 69 students (69 % of intake), which is below the legal limit, so the university must stop further transfers at that point.  \n\nThus, the legal stop point is reached **the moment the projected post‑transfer enrollment would fall under 70 % of the branch’s sanctioned intake**."
      }
    ],
    "lastAccessed": 1775158829060
  },
